Totally understandable — database queries can get unwieldy fast. A great way to clean them up is to move them into a Repository pattern, which centralizes all your data access logic in one place.

Here's a simple example in Python with SQLAlchemy:

```python
class UserRepository:
    def __init__(self, session):
        self.session = session

    def get_by_id(self, user_id: int):
        return self.session.query(User).filter(User.id == user_id).first()

    def get_active_users(self):
        return self.session.query(User).filter(User.is_active == True).all()

    def create(self, user_data: dict):
        user = User(**user_data)
        self.session.add(user)
        self.session.commit()
        return user
```

This keeps your queries out of your business logic and makes them much easier to test and maintain.

